what's up youtubes welcome to my channel  Richard on data my name is Richard and  this is the channel where we talk about  all things data related so I want to  talk to you guys today about what a data  scientist actually is because it's a  very amorphous and nebulous term there's  not generally a universally agreed-upon  definition of it and honestly if you  apply to about twelve different jobs  you're probably gonna see fifteen  different job descriptions for it so the  data scientist has been called the  sexiest job of the 21st century and  there's no question that it's one of the  hottest jobs on the market right now  it's also incredibly well-paid a recent  study that was done on this found that  somebody fresh out of school starting in  a data science job is looking at a  median salary of about 95 thousand  dollars a year and then similarly once  you work your way up and start getting  some years experience you start looking  at somewhere around 128 thousand dollars  a year as an average salary now granted  these are just averages obviously these  numbers are gonna be a little higher if  you're in an area with a higher cost of  living but one way or the other you're  gonna be making a pretty good living if  you're a data scientist but what exactly  are the skills required to be a data  scientist well I hate to break it to you  guys but in order to have any chance of  being a data scientist you need to know  our Python Julia sequel skaila Java C C  sharp C++ objective-c and Fortran you  also need to know several business  intelligence technologies so something  like power bi something like tableau you  need to know a lot of Big Data  technology so Hadoop spark MapReduce  also you need to be incredibly well  educated with at least a PhD and you  need to know backwards and forwards  statistics machine learning deep  learning and linear algebra wrong  that's so completely wrong so honestly  you're gonna see some jobs out there the  have these giant lists of requirements  like that but even in those jobs I would  wager a guess the majority of people are  not using those technologies at least  every single one of them every single  day and it's really more of a red flag  that  he doesn't really know what they're  doing from a data standpoint and they're  looking from summer for some magical  outsider to come in and fix all their  data problems so it's really a red flag  that company doesn't really know what  they're doing so before I get into the  core skills that a data scientist has  just a little bit about me so I've been  a data scientist for about five years  now and an hour programmer for about  eight years that's been through a  variety of small companies and large  companies in industry as well as in  academia and through my experience this  is typically the skills that a data  scientist is gonna have to focus on from  day to day now keep in mind I also live  in the Midwest region of the US so if  you're in Silicon Valley or the Bay Area  generally you may have a slightly  different experience because you might  be expected to be on the cutting edge of  data science every day but for your  average Joe run-of-the-mill person just  looking to sharpen their skills and  become a data scientist I think  generally this is gonna be the case so  data science is an incredibly  multidisciplinary field so as a result a  data scientist is often gonna feel a lot  more like a jack-of-all-trades as  opposed to a master of none sort of  thing so it's often helpful to think  about a Venn diagram like this to  summarize the core skills that a data  scientist needs to know and is going to  use every single day so those skill sets  are domain expertise statistics  programming capabilities and  communication and granted there are a  lot of variants on this Venn diagram and  you'll probably see different ones  floating around there out there but to  me this is the most accurate framework  for thinking through what a data  scientist really is so if we start over  here with domain knowledge so that's  basically your understanding of the  physical domain in which the data are  actually generated so let's take  healthcare for example so in healthcare  you typically work with like outpatient  clinics inpatient hospitals and they  have some kind of electronic medical  record or electronic healthcare records  system where your data actually live in  now there's a business process on the  back end which actually generates that  data and really understanding what that  business process looks like is going to  cause you to ask  fundamental questions and really guide  your creative problem-solving process  it's also important to be able to speak  the same language as your stakeholders  as well and what I mean by that is  typically you're going to be working  with people who aren't data people  they're not data scientists they've  never worked with a programming language  in their life but you're trying to give  them some kind of business insight so  it's often easy if you don't know the  domain very well to find something that  you think is really exciting you go to  share it with them and then they just  don't care because that's not the world  that they live in so for that reason  understanding that actual domain is very  very important  unfortunately it's difficult for me to  help you guys out with domain knowledge  because I've only worked with healthcare  and pharma and a couple little ones here  and there myself so I don't know all of  them in and out having said that it's  generally been my experience that once  you start immersing yourself in the  domain you end up getting pretty  acquainted with it after about 6 to 12  months after the 12 month mark or so you  start really being able to sit down have  really solid business development  related conversations now moving over to  the next one we have statistics so  statistics to me is really the science  behind data science now if you had the  other three skills but you're lacking in  statistics  you're probably dabbling in what I like  to call betas pseudoscience  so dates the distance is really about  providing some intuition and some  mathematical backbone behind what you're  doing and you can loosely couple that  skill set together with things like  mathematics linear algebra machine  learning but broadly speaking that skill  set consists of things like knowing what  statistical tests are appropriate for  your problem having some sound  foundation for claims that you may be  making making appropriate conclusions  and then knowing things like a Cox model  linear regression logistic regression  general things like that and now I  should also point out some people think  a data scientist is just a rebranded  statistician and to a certain extent I  do understand that point the term data  scientist is a pretty recent creation  and statistician is something that's  been around for several decades but to  me  you think of that overall skillset as a  statistician to me that really  overemphasizes this part and yeah  obviously it's very important but it's  only one part of this whole  comprehensive framework where all four  parts are incredibly important moving on  to the next item on the list we have  programming capabilities so in the data  science world your core programming  languages are typically R and Python now  in this video I'm not going to get into  which of those two is better or if you  know neither of them which one should  you learn but really the important point  that I want to stress is that working in  a programming language is absolutely  critical and the reason for that is  generally as a data scientist you're  going to be working with other people  now that could be like I said before  people who don't speak the same language  as you as far as a domain but oftentimes  it's going to be other analysts and  other data scientists so being able to  share your work and having that work be  something that's reproducible is going  to be critically important the beauty of  working with a programming language is  also that you have a reproducible paper  trail so you're gonna start from your  raw data and then as you work from your  raw data into some kind of deliverable  that deliverable could be something like  a model it could be a report it could be  a visualization it could be an app but  that code basically shows exactly how  you got from point A to point B that's  really how you show your work similarly  having a reproducible programming  language enables you to do one  functionality and then maybe you're  going to repeat that functionality  multiple times so you have to do it  again for a separate project or for a  separate analysis but because you've  already created that functionality you  can reuse it on your later work so it  ends up saving you a ton of time there's  also a joke in data science that often  times 80% of your time is spent cleaning  data and your other 20% of your time to  spend complaining about cleaning data  and I can tell you there's definitely a  modest element of truth to that but that  data cleaning I can't stress enough that  part's gonna happen in the programming  language that you're working with and  then maybe you get to do something  exciting like building the model or  building a rapport but that part should  also take place inside of that same  Grahame language so moving to the last  item on this list we have communication  skills now that's pretty broad and  there's a lot to unpack behind that one  but if we think about the core  deliverables which you may be working on  as a data scientist let's say it's a  report well you need to be able to put  together a well-written report is it  understandable do you have good language  skills so things like that if it's a  visualization there are actually what's  called tough days graphical principles  for best design of a visualization and  that makes your visualization easy to  read understandable and all that jazz  and similarly if you have an app and  that's your deliverable there are best  overall design principles which you  should be able to to use and now  similarly to you're probably going to be  working in a multidisciplinary  environment so you may have to actually  get along with other data scientists and  write code with them and communicate  with them on the day to day you need to  solve your problem together with your  client and with your stakeholder and  similarly you're gonna have to work  probably in your own company with things  like product owners product managers  generally people who are there to help  you but they don't have the same  background or the same skill set that  you have  so the overall communication skills of  being able to compartmentalize the  technical aspects and the data side of  things and speak the same language as  other people from different backgrounds  that's gonna be something you need to do  on the day-to-day so putting it all  together once you have all four of these  skills you've got signs specifically  data signs and now obviously the  overwhelming majority of people they're  gonna be really good at one or two of  these things and they're really gonna  struggle at one or two of these other  things and that's absolutely okay it's  really important to be able to identify  where you're actually struggling and  then making improvements at it but  really understanding how all four these  things fits together and then actually  being able to work on them and get  better  that's really the course being a good  day of scientist it's worth pointing out  though that the term data science can be  a little bit misleading in fact  sometimes I like to call it data art  rather than data science because it's  actually  tremendously creative field and often  you're not going to be a task taker or a  programmer but you're going to be a  creative problem solver that as a client  could come up to you and say that they  want X but it is kind of your job to  nudge them a little bit and point out  that a solution Y could be offered so  maybe you end up delivering them some  combination of X and y or you end up  delivering them just Y after you explain  why that's better but often that  creative aspect of it and then making  them believe in the first place that Y  is their idea and it's the best solution  so that creative aspect is often  completely where your value is gonna  come from when you work in data science  thanks so much for watching this video  if you enjoyed this video  smash the like button and also hit the  subscribe button it's my plan to use  this channel to upload all kinds of  educational content to help you guys  either become a data scientist for the  first time ever or sharpen the skills  that you already have developed so until  next time richer on data

